% A CHR benchmark (not run by `dune test`): the primes up to N by a sieve
% of constraints.  Each number J is a constraint prime J K; the rule
% removes a multiple J of a smaller I.  All the constraints are suspended
% on the same variable K, so that the rule sees each pair: 60 169
% attempts for N = 300.  (On _, as Elpi allows, the interpreter would suspend each on
% a variable of its own: README, "Limits".)

func prime int, any.
constraint prime {
  rule (prime I K) \ (prime J K) | (0 is J mod I) <=> true.
}
prime J K :- declare_constraint (prime J K) [K].

% [primes N]: the constraints prime 2 K .. prime N K; those left are the primes.
pred primes i:int.
primes N :- candidates N _.

pred candidates i:int, i:any.
candidates N _ :- N < 2, !.
candidates N K :- prime N K, M is N - 1, candidates M K.
